<GuideLayout
  slug="bathtub-refinishing-cost"
  title="Bathtub Refinishing Cost: Typical Prices & What Affects It"
  description="What bathtub refinishing (reglazing) typically costs, what changes the price, refinishing vs. replacing, and how to get an accurate quote."
  heading="How Much Does Bathtub Refinishing Cost?"
  lede="Typical price ranges for bathtub refinishing and reglazing, what pushes a quote up or down, and when replacing the tub makes more sense."
  datePublished="2026-10-30"
  faqs={faqs}
>
  <p>Bathtub refinishing (often called <strong>reglazing</strong>) restores a worn, stained, or dated tub by repairing the surface and spraying on a new bonded coating. Because nothing is torn out, it usually costs a small fraction of replacing the tub.</p>

  <p>The ranges below are typical national price ranges, meant as a starting point. Prices vary by region, contractor, and the condition of your tub, so treat them as estimates, not a quote. For an exact price on your tub, call {phone}.</p>

  <h2>Typical Bathtub Refinishing Prices</h2>

  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Job</th><th>Typical range*</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Standard built-in tub (porcelain, enamel-on-steel, or fiberglass)</td><td>About $350–$650</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Tub plus tile surround</td><td>Higher than tub-only; depends on how much tile there is</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Clawfoot or freestanding cast iron tub</td><td>More than a standard tub; the exterior and feet add time</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Tub replacement (for comparison)</td><td>About $1,500–$5,000+ installed</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p class="text-sm">*Typical national ranges that vary by area and contractor. Not a quote.</p>

  <div class="callout">
    <p><strong>Bottom line:</strong> for most standard tubs, refinishing costs a few hundred dollars, while replacement often runs into the thousands once removal, plumbing, and wall repair are included.</p>
  </div>

  <h2>What Changes the Price</h2>

  <h3>Tub size and material</h3>
  <p>A standard 5-foot alcove tub is the baseline. Larger soaking tubs, garden tubs, and whirlpool tubs have more surface to prep and coat. Porcelain on cast iron, enamel on steel, and fiberglass or acrylic all refinish well, but each needs slightly different prep, which can shift the price a little.</p>

  <h3>Chips, cracks, and repairs</h3>
  <p>A few small chips are often filled as part of a normal job. Deep chips, rust spots around the drain, cracked fiberglass, or a surface that was refinished before and is now peeling take extra time. Old, failing coatings usually have to be stripped first, which adds to the cost.</p>

  <h3>Color</h3>
  <p>White and almond are the most common choices. Custom colors or a color change can cost more because the coating has to be tinted and fully cover the old color. If you are matching existing fixtures or tile, mention it when you call.</p>

  <h3>Tile surround and other surfaces</h3>
  <p>Refinishing the wall tile around the tub is priced separately because it is a lot more surface, with grout lines to fill and seal. Doing the tub and surround in one visit is usually cheaper than two separate jobs. Sinks, shower pans, and countertops can often be added the same day.</p>

  <h3>Clawfoot and freestanding tubs</h3>
  <p>A clawfoot tub needs the interior done like any other tub, and many owners also want the exterior and feet painted or refinished. The exterior is curved and needs careful masking, so expect it to cost more than a standard built-in tub.</p>

  <h3>Access and condition of the bathroom</h3>
  <p>Tight bathrooms, poor ventilation, or caulk and fixtures that need to be removed and replaced can add time. Fresh caulk is usually part of the job; replacing faucets or drains is not.</p>

  <h2>Refinishing vs. Replacing a Bathtub</h2>

  <p>Replacing a tub is rarely just the price of the new tub. A typical replacement includes tearing out the old tub, plumbing changes, repairing the wall and tile around it, and sometimes flooring, which is why installed prices commonly land around $1,500 to $5,000 or more. Refinishing leaves the tub, plumbing, and walls in place.</p>

  <p><strong>Refinishing usually makes sense when:</strong></p>
  <ul>
    <li>The tub is structurally sound but stained, dull, chipped, or an outdated color</li>
    <li>You have a heavy cast iron tub that is hard and expensive to remove</li>
    <li>You are updating a rental or getting a house ready to sell on a budget</li>
    <li>You like the tub's size and layout and just want it to look new</li>
  </ul>

  <p><strong>Replacing may be the better choice when:</strong></p>
  <ul>
    <li>The tub leaks, flexes, or has large structural cracks</li>
    <li>You want a different size, shape, or a walk-in shower instead</li>
    <li>You are already gutting the bathroom down to the studs</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>How Long a Refinished Tub Lasts</h2>

  <p>With proper care, a refinished bathtub can last 10 to 15 years. How the finish is treated matters more than anything else:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Wait the full 24 to 48 hours before using the tub</li>
    <li>Clean with mild, non-abrasive cleaners and a soft cloth or sponge</li>
    <li>Skip suction-cup bath mats, which can pull at the coating</li>
    <li>Fix dripping faucets so water does not sit on the finish</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>How Long the Job Takes</h2>

  <p>Most bathtubs are refinished in 3 to 5 hours, and showers and tile take about 4 to 6 hours. Plan on not using the tub for 24 to 48 hours while the coating cures. There is no demolition, so the rest of the bathroom stays usable.</p>

  <h2>How to Get an Accurate Quote</h2>

  <p>Most bathtub refinishing quotes can be given over the phone if you have a few details ready:</p>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Tub type:</strong> built-in, clawfoot, garden, or whirlpool, and the material if you know it</li>
    <li><strong>Condition:</strong> chips, cracks, rust, stains, or an old coating that is peeling</li>
    <li><strong>Color:</strong> standard white or almond, or a custom color</li>
    <li><strong>Extras:</strong> tile surround, sink, shower pan, or countertop on the same visit</li>
    <li><strong>Photos:</strong> a couple of clear photos help when the condition is hard to describe</li>
  </ol>

  <p>Ask what the price includes: chip repair, caulk, stripping any old coating, and how long you should wait before using the tub. A clear quote should spell those out before work starts.</p>

  <p>We provide <a href="/">bathtub reglazing and refinishing</a> across Northern Kentucky and the greater Cincinnati area, including <a href="/bath/florence-ky/">Florence</a>, <a href="/bath/covington-ky/">Covington</a>, <a href="/bath/fort-thomas-ky/">Fort Thomas</a>, and <a href="/bath/newport-ky/">Newport</a>. Call {phone} for a free estimate.</p>
